{"language":"English","entry_date":"2026-11-16T09:33:31Z","relation":"Journal Name: Applied Catalysis B: Environment and Energy Journal Volume: 403","report_number":"BNL-230433-2026-JAAM","identifier":"SC0012704","country_publication":"United States","journal_type":"AM","doi":"10.1016/j.apcatb.2026.127584","description":"Direct ammonia protonic ceramic fuel cells (DA-PCFCs) represent a promising platform for efficient, low-emission power generation at intermediate temperatures; however, their performance is often constrained by sluggish anodic reaction kinetics. Herein, we demonstrate an active and durable DA-PCFC employing a self-assembled, heterostructured Ni-BaZr0.1Ce0.7Y0.1Yb0.1O3−δ (Ni-BZCYYb) cermet anode, decorated with in situ formed NiCo alloy and MoO3−δ catalyst under operating conditions. The resulting three-phase heterointerfaces synergistically enhance ammonia adsorption, activation, and decomposition kinetics. The cell delivers peak power densities of 0.464, 0.713, 1.051, and 1.566 W cm−2 at 550, 600, 650, and 700 °C, respectively, and exhibits stable operation for 100 h at 600 °C under a constant current density of 0.5 A cm−2. Density functional theory calculations identify the NiCo-MoO3−δ interface as the primary active site, providing favorable adsorption energetics and reduced reaction barriers. These findings establish an effective strategy for developing efficient electrocatalysts for high-performance, intermediate-temperature DA-PCFCs.","title":"In-situ self-assembled NiCo-MoO3-δ heterostructured catalysts for high-performance direct ammonia protonic ceramic fuel cells","other_number":"Journal ID: ISSN 0926-3373","contract_number":"SC0012704","publication_date":"2027-04-01T00:00:00Z","journal_volume":"403","osti_id":"17428540","journal_name":"Applied Catalysis B: Environment and Energy","product_type":"Journal Article","authors":["Chen, Liyan","Sasaki, Kotaro (ORCID:0000000324748323)","He, Fan","Yang, Xuan","Zhu, Feng","Hu, Feng","Sasaki, Kotaro","Choi, YongMan","Chen, Yu"],"subjects":["30 DIRECT ENERGY CONVERSION","Activity","Ammonia decomposition catalyst","Durability","Protonic ceramic fuel cells"],"article_type":"Accepted Manuscript","doe_contract_number":"SC0012704","sponsor_orgs":["Brookhaven National Lab","Office of Critical Minerals and Energy Innovation (CMEI), Hydrogen Fuel Cell Technologies Office (HFTO)"],"research_orgs":["Brookhaven National Lab"],"journal_issn":"ISSN 0926-3373","other_identifiers":["Journal ID: ISSN 0926-3373"],"links":[{"rel":"citation","href":"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/17428540"},{"rel":"fulltext","href":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/17428540"},{"rel":"citation_doe_pages","href":"https://www.osti.gov/pages/biblio/17428540"}]},

{"language":"English","entry_date":"2026-11-23T10:19:41Z","relation":"Journal Name: Applied Catalysis B: Environment and Energy Journal Volume: 404","report_number":"BNL-230437-2026-JAAM","identifier":"SC0012704","country_publication":"United States","journal_type":"AM","doi":"10.1016/j.apcatb.2026.127711","title":"Simplicity and completeness in CuOx/CeO2 catalyst synthesis via one-pot chemical vapor deposition for efficient CO2 conversion via RWGS reaction","other_number":"Journal ID: ISSN 0926-3373","contract_number":"SC0012704","publication_date":"2027-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal_volume":"404","osti_id":"17429768","journal_name":"Applied Catalysis B: Environment and Energy","product_type":"Journal Article","authors":["Pophali, Amol","Kwon, Gihan (ORCID:0000000279632136)","Lee, Chi Ho","Lee, Geon-Hyeong","Roh, Jangeon","Kwon, Gihan","Yoon, Kwangsuk","Hwang, Sooyeon","Hollyer, George G.","Marcella, Nicholas","Song, Hocheol","Lee, Yeol-Lim","Kim, Do Heui","Tong, Xiao","Stach, Eric A.","Kwon, Joseph Sang-Il","Kim, Taejin"],"subjects":["36 MATERIALS SCIENCE"],"article_type":"Accepted Manuscript","doe_contract_number":"SC0012704","sponsor_orgs":["Brookhaven National Lab","USDOE Office of Science (SC), Basic Energy Sciences (BES). Scientific User Facilities (SUF)"],"research_orgs":["Brookhaven National Lab"],"journal_issn":"ISSN 0926-3373","other_identifiers":["Journal ID: ISSN 0926-3373"],"links":[{"rel":"citation","href":"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/17429768"},{"rel":"fulltext","href":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/17429768"},{"rel":"citation_doe_pages","href":"https://www.osti.gov/pages/biblio/17429768"}]},

{"language":"English","entry_date":"2026-09-24T11:25:59Z","relation":"Journal Name: Applied Catalysis. B, Environmental Journal Volume: 401","identifier":"AC05-00OR22725; EE0010304","nondoe_contract_number":"FWP FEAA421","country_publication":"United States","publisher":"Elsevier","journal_type":"AM","doi":"10.1016/j.apcatb.2026.127268","description":"Zeolites serve as essential catalytic platforms for many industrial processes, including emerging ethanol-to-olefins (ETO) upgrading technologies. Although metal-loaded (Cu, Zn, Y) dealuminated beta (deAlBeta) zeolite powders are promising catalysts for direct ETO conversion with high selectivity to butene-rich C<sub>3+<\/sub> olefins necessary for production of sustainable aviation fuels (SAF), development of these materials as shaped technical bodies through the incorporation of binders is required for scale-up and commercial viability. Here, we report the ethanol upgrading performance of Cu-Zn-Y/deAlBeta extruded catalysts formulated with either alumina or kaolin clay binders. Both extrudates exhibit high ethanol dehydration reactivity which competes with the initial ethanol dehydrogenation step in the direct ETO reaction network. Consequently, elevated selectivity to dehydration side products (ethylene, diethyl ether) at ∼100% ethanol conversion is observed on Cu-Zn-Y/deAlBeta extrudates compared to the powder catalyst, which inhibits production of desired C<sub>3+<\/sub> olefins. Utilizing microscopy and spectroscopic characterizations, we attribute this to Al migration from binder to zeolite particles within the extrudates, thus re-aluminating the zeolite and generating Brønsted acid sites active for dehydration reactions. In conclusion, this work elucidates the effects of binder incorporation on ETO product distributions and emphasizes that binder selection must be carefully considered during design of extruded zeolite catalysts.","title":"Migration of Al within dealuminated beta extruded catalysts influences olefin distribution during ethanol upgrading","other_number":"Journal ID: ISSN 0926-3373","contract_number":"AC05-00OR22725; EE0010304","publication_date":"2027-02-01T00:00:00Z","journal_volume":"401","osti_id":"3413717","journal_name":"Applied Catalysis. B, Environmental","product_type":"Journal Article","authors":["Jacobs, Hunter P. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:000000016190874X)","Borate, Shivangi N. [University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL (United States)]","Hur, Young Gul [University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL (United States)]","Foucher, Alexandre C. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0000000150424002)","Li, Meijun [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0000000221648629)","Oyedeji, Oluwafemi A. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:000000028684425X)","Rossy, Andrés Márquez [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0009000746357194)","Kidder, Michelle K. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:000000030851835X)","Kimpiab, Elyse E. [University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL (United States)]","Zornes, Anya [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0009000181192803)","Damron, Joshua T. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)]","Cullen, David A. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0000000225937866)","Harris, James W. [University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL (United States)] (ORCID:0000000167609951)","Sutton, Andrew D. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0000000179841715)","Purdy, Stephen C. [Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), Oak Ridge, TN (United States)] (ORCID:0000000298701029)"],"subjects":["Binders","Brønsted acidity","Ethanol","Extrudates","Olefins","Sustainable aviation fuel","Zeolites"],"